<tr class="table-body-row">
  <td class="table-body-cell">
    <%# items-start, and a 40px logo at every width.
        design.md: body cells, action buttons and checkboxes all top-align to the row's *first
        line*. This cell was `items-center` around a logo that grew to 64px at lg, so the ticker
        sat 13px below the top of its own row while the price and holdings text sat at 12px, and
        the taller the logo the further they diverged. 40px is exactly the height of the two-line
        ticker-over-company block, so the logo no longer sets the row height at all. %>
    <div class="flex items-start gap-3">
      <% logo_path = "stock_logos/#{stock.ticker}-logo.svg" %>
      <%# `alt=""`, because the ticker is printed immediately beside this and a screen reader would
          otherwise say it twice - "AAPL logo, AAPL, Apple Inc.". `portfolios/show` already renders the
          same logo that way and says so; this one said "AAPL logo". %>
      <% if File.exist?("app/assets/images/#{logo_path}") %>
        <%= image_tag asset_path(logo_path),
                      class: "size-10 shrink-0 rounded-lg",
                      alt: "",
                      loading: "lazy" %>
      <% end %>
      <div class="flex flex-col">
        <%# text-lg font-semibold made the ticker the largest text on the page after the h1,
          in a cell whose neighbours are text-sm. A table's primary identifier is body size with
          medium weight - Tailwind UI, Polaris and Primer all render it that way. %>
        <%# Line one is the security: the ticker and the market it trades on. The header above has said
            "Company (exchange)" since it was written and this cell had never contained an exchange -
            `stock_exchange` is populated on every active stock and was rendered only on stocks#show. A
            ticker without its market is ambiguous, which is why Bloomberg, Yahoo Finance and Google
            Finance all render the pair. %>
        <span class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-1.5">
          <%= link_to stock.ticker, stock_path(stock), class: "tw-link" %>
          <% if (market = stock_market_note(stock)) %>
            <span class="text-xs text-slate-600">&middot; <%= market %></span>
          <% end %>
        </span>

        <%# Line two is the company: its name, and what it does.
            **Industry is here rather than in a column of its own**, which is a change from the preview,
            and the reason is measurable. It is populated on all ten active stocks and none of the eight
            archived ones, so a column would be empty on every archived row - and worse, a column present
            on one table and absent on the other gives the two stacked tables different geometry, which
            moves every column at the boundary and steps the page sideways. In the cell it costs nothing
            when absent, needs no width, and reads as what it is: a fact about the company, on the
            company's line. %>
        <span class="text-sm text-slate-600">
          <%= stock.company_name %><% if stock.industry.present? %> &middot; <%= stock.industry %><% end %>
        </span>

        <%# What "archived" means, for the one table where it is not obvious. There is no
            archived_at column - `archived` is a bare boolean - so the date shown is
            last_trading_day, which the price job maintains and which is the more useful figure
            anyway: it says when this price was last real. Omitted when the job has never seen the
            stock, rather than printing a blank. %>
        <% if stock.archived? %>
          <span class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600">
            No longer trading<% if stock.last_trading_day.present? %> &middot; last priced <%= stock.last_trading_day.strftime("%-d %b %Y") %><% end %>
          </span>
        <% end %>

        <%# Below lg the holdings count and the trade buttons live here, in the one cell that is
            always on screen. As their own trailing columns they sat at left=370 in a viewport
            326px wide - the trading floor's only call to action was off the right edge of a
            horizontal scroll on a phone, which is the width most students are on. %>
        <%# Below lg the Held by figure moves into this cell, exactly as a student's holdings line does -
            the trailing columns are `hidden` there, and a teacher on a phone would otherwise be back to
            the two-column page this column exists to fix. %>
        <% if policy(stock).show_class_holdings? && @class_students&.positive? %>
          <% held = @class_holdings&.dig(stock.id) %>
          <span class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 lg:hidden">
            <% if held %>
              Held by <%= held[:holders] %> of <%= @class_students %> &middot;
              <%= shares_label(held[:shares]) %>
            <% else %>
              Nobody owns this yet
            <% end %>
          </span>
        <% end %>

        <% if policy(stock).show_holdings? %>
          <span class="mt-1 text-xs text-slate-600 lg:hidden">
            You own <%= share_count(@portfolio&.shares_owned(stock.id) || 0) %>
          </span>

          <% if policy(stock).show_trading_link? %>
            <%= render "stocks/trade_actions", stock: stock, class: "mt-2 lg:hidden" %>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    </div>
  </td>
  <%# The price, and how old it is.

      **Stale means "older than the freshest price on this page"**, which needs no calendar knowledge - and
      that matters, because the job runs at 02:00 for the *previous* close, so a perfectly fresh price
      normally carries yesterday's date. Comparing against today would mark every row.

      When the whole page is behind, no row is marked and the page's own description carries the date, which
      is the honest way round: "prices as of 3 August" is the fact, not "every company is stale".

      slate-600, not amber or rose: a price that has not been refreshed is a fact about the data, not an
      error the student has made or a warning about the company. %>
  <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right">
    <span class="table-cell-content block">
      <%= number_to_currency(stock.current_price) %>
    </span>

    <% if stock.last_trading_day.blank? %>
      <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-slate-600">Not priced yet</span>
    <% elsif local_assigns[:freshest_price_on].present? && stock.last_trading_day < freshest_price_on %>
      <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-slate-600">
        as of <%= stock.last_trading_day.strftime("%-d %b") %>
      </span>
    <% end %>
  </td>

  <%# Same condition as the header in _stocks_table, so the column counts cannot diverge. It used
      to be current_user.student? there and policy(stock).show_holdings? here - a student with no
      persisted portfolio got four header cells over two body cells. %>
  <% if policy(stock).show_holdings? %>
    <td class="table-body-cell hidden lg:table-cell">
      <span class="table-cell-content">
        <%= share_count(@portfolio&.shares_owned(stock.id) || 0) %>
      </span>
    </td>
    <%# `align-top`, so a 40px control sits on the row's first line instead of on its centre.
        Measured before: the primary cell stacks a 40px logo over a ticker and a company name, so the row
        is 65px and the buttons' centre landed 10.5px below the ticker. The `pt-1.5` used for a 32px row
        action is the wrong number here and any number would be - it is tuned to one row's height and
        breaks the moment a company name wraps to a third line. design.md already asks for top alignment
        on every other trailing cell. %>
    <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right hidden whitespace-nowrap align-top lg:table-cell">
      <% if policy(stock).show_trading_link? %>
        <%= render "stocks/trade_actions", stock: stock, class: "justify-end" %>
      <% end %>
    </td>
  <% end %>

  <%# Who owns this, among the people this viewer can see. Same condition as the header in
      _stocks_table, so the column counts cannot diverge - that has happened here before, when one used
      current_user.student? and the other a policy.

      A figure, not a sentence: "9 of 25 students" repeated down a column is twenty-five copies of the
      denominator, so the word lives in the header and the section's description says which twenty-five.
      Zero is stated rather than left blank - nobody owning a stock is information, and an empty cell
      reads as missing data. %>
  <% if policy(stock).show_class_holdings? && @class_students&.positive? %>
    <% held = @class_holdings&.dig(stock.id) %>
    <td class="table-body-cell table-body-cell-right hidden lg:table-cell">
      <% if held %>
        <span class="table-cell-content block tabular-nums">
          <%= held[:holders] %> of <%= @class_students %>
        </span>
        <span class="mt-0.5 block text-xs text-slate-600 tabular-nums">
          <%= shares_label(held[:shares]) %>
        </span>
      <% else %>
        <span class="table-cell-content block text-slate-600">None</span>
      <% end %>
    </td>
  <% end %>
</tr>
